## Introduction
DSU is a tree used to deal with merging and searching problems within non-intersecting sets. For example, we can use DSU to deal with problems like how many connected blocks are there in a graph, does a node belong to a connected block, are two nodes in a connected block, etc. A DSU is usually made up with an int array `pre[]` and two functions `find()` and `unite()`.

## How it works
`pre[i]` is used to storage `i`'s father node, and function `find()` is used to search the root node of a node. So, if two nodes `a` and `b` shares the same result of `find()` (`find(a) = find(b)`), then they belong to one connected block.
According to the principle of `find()`, if it needs to connect two nodes using the function `unite()`, then it only needs to run `pre[find(b)] = find(a)`.
**Note: The `pre` array needs initialization so that $pre_i=i$, as the base case of function `find()`.**

## The template of function`find()` and `unite()`
```cpp
bool find(int k){ // search for a node's root
	if(find(k) == k){ // pre_i = i，decursion ends, k is the No. of the root node
		return k;
	}
	return find(pre[k]); // search for pre_i's father
}
```

```cpp
void unite(int a, int b){ // merge the connected blocks a&b belong to
	a = find(a);
	b = find(b); // process each's roots
	if(a == b) return; // a&b are already connected
	pre[b] = a; // connect, b is not a root again
}
```

Template Problem: [Luogu P3367-【Template】DSU](https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P3367)
